
1) How do you give a developer temporary access to S3 for 24 hours?
Idea: Create an IAM role that grants the needed S3 permissions and let the developer assume it for a limited duration (24 hours) using STS.
Why: Roles + STS give temporary credentials, no long-lived keys.

Example (high-level):

In Account A (where S3 lives), create role DevTempS3Role with trust allowing the developer’s principal (or account) to assume it.

Attach a policy to the role that allows S3 actions on the required bucket.

Developer runs aws sts assume-role --role-arn ... --duration-seconds 86400 to get temporary creds (86400 sec = 24 hours).

Role trust policy (JSON):

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::111122223333:user/dev.jane" },
    "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
  }]
}
Role permissions policy (JSON):

{
  "Version":"2012-10-17",
  "Statement":[{
    "Effect":"Allow",
    "Action":["s3:GetObject","s3:ListBucket","s3:PutObject"],
    "Resource":["arn:aws:s3:::example-bucket","arn:aws:s3:::example-bucket/*"]
  }]
}
CLI to assume role:

aws sts assume-role \
  --role-arn arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A:role/DevTempS3Role \
  --role-session-name dev-temp \
  --duration-seconds 86400
Best practice: Use MFA or require approval for sensitive data and use least privilege (only needed operations + prefixes).

2) How do you allow EC2 to securely access S3 without using access keys?
Answer: Attach an IAM role (instance profile) to the EC2 instance. Code on EC2 uses the instance metadata service to obtain temporary credentials automatically.

Why: Avoids embedding access keys on instances; credentials are rotated automatically by AWS.

Example:

Create IAM role EC2S3ReadRole with policy:

{
  "Version":"2012-10-17",
  "Statement":[{
    "Effect":"Allow",
    "Action":["s3:GetObject","s3:ListBucket"],
    "Resource":["arn:aws:s3:::my-data","arn:aws:s3:::my-data/*"]
  }]
}
Attach that role to the EC2 instance (via Launch Template / Console / CLI).

On the instance, AWS SDKs automatically pick credentials (no manual config).

CLI to attach (example for existing instance):

aws ec2 associate-iam-instance-profile --instance-id i-0abc123 --iam-instance-profile Name=EC2S3ReadRole
Best practice: Use least privilege and restrict actions to S3 prefixes.

3) A user can log in but still cannot access an S3 bucket. What would you check?
Checklist & steps (ordered):

Explicit Deny — check bucket policy for explicit Deny (explicit deny overrides allow).

Resource-based policy — check S3 bucket policy and object ACLs.

IAM policies — check user’s attached policies, group policies, and managed policies.

Permission boundaries — a permission boundary on the principal could limit effective permissions.

Service Control Policies (SCPs) (if AWS Organizations used) — SCP can block actions account-wide.

Session policies — if using AssumeRole, session policies could restrict.

Bucket encryption / KMS — if bucket uses SSE-KMS, check KMS key policy allows the user to kms:Decrypt.

VPC endpoint policy — if access is via an S3 VPC Endpoint, the endpoint policy may restrict access.

AWS CloudTrail / Access Denied logs — check CloudTrail for error events to see the denied action and principal.

Tools: IAM Policy Simulator, S3 Access Analyzer, CloudTrail logs.

4) How do you enforce MFA for console and CLI access?
Console MFA enforcement: Attach an IAM policy that denies all console actions unless aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent is true.

Policy example (console & API protected):

{
  "Version":"2012-10-17",
  "Statement":[
    {
      "Effect":"Deny",
      "Action":"*",
      "Resource":"*",
      "Condition":{"Bool":{"aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent":"false"}}
    }
  ]
}
Apply only to the users/groups or as a user-level policy (careful: make sure at least one administrator can bypass temporarily).

CLI/API with MFA: Clients must get temporary credentials using sts:GetSessionToken (or sts:AssumeRole) providing MFA; then these temp creds are used for CLI/API.

CLI to get temp creds with MFA:

aws sts get-session-token \
  --serial-number arn:aws:iam::123456789012:mfa/dev.jane \
  --token-code 123456 \
  --duration-seconds 3600
Then export the returned AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID, AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, AWS_SESSION_TOKEN.

Best practice: Require MFA for privileged users/roles; use hardware or virtual MFA and rotate backup admin.

5) How do you share access with another AWS account securely?
Answer: Use cross-account IAM roles (recommended) or resource-based policies.

Approach 1 — Cross-account role (most common):

In Account B (resource/account owner) create role CrossAccountAccessRole whose trust policy allows Account A (the calling account) to assume the role.

Attach appropriate permissions to the role (e.g., S3 read).

From Account A, an IAM user or role calls sts:AssumeRole to get temporary credentials.

Trust policy example (role in Account B):

{
 "Version":"2012-10-17",
 "Statement":[{
   "Effect":"Allow",
   "Principal":{"AWS":"arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/DevTeamRole"},
   "Action":"sts:AssumeRole"
 }]
}
Approach 2 — Resource-based policy: For S3 you can add a bucket policy that allows principals from another account to access it (useful for simple read/write sharing).

Best practice: Use roles + least privilege and audit via CloudTrail.

6) What’s the best way to give read-only access to CloudWatch logs?
Answer: Use AWS managed policy CloudWatchLogsReadOnlyAccess or create a custom policy limited to logs:Describe*, logs:GetLogEvents, logs:FilterLogEvents.

Custom example:

{
  "Version":"2012-10-17",
  "Statement":[{
    "Effect":"Allow",
    "Action":[
      "logs:DescribeLogGroups",
      "logs:DescribeLogStreams",
      "logs:GetLogEvents",
      "logs:FilterLogEvents",
      "logs:DescribeSubscriptionFilters"
    ],
    "Resource":"*"
  }]
}
Best practice: Narrow Resource to specific log groups where possible.

7) How do you enforce that all IAM users rotate access keys every 90 days?
Options:

AWS Config managed rule iam-credentials-check or a custom AWS Config rule that flags keys older than 90 days.

Automated Lambda — run periodic Lambda that reads the IAM credential report, finds keys older than 90 days and disables or emails owners.

IAM policy enforcement — you cannot directly force a rotation with a single IAM policy; policies can deny actions unless using temporary/session credentials (encourage role usage).

Organizational policy + automation — enforce using Guardrails and automated remediations.

Example automation: AWS Config with iam-access-keys-rotated or a scheduled Lambda that:

Uses aws iam get-credential-report

Parses access_key_1_last_rotated, access_key_2_last_rotated

Notifies or disables keys past 90 days.

Best practice: Prefer temporary credentials (roles, SSO) to reduce reliance on long-lived access keys.

8) How do you troubleshoot when an IAM policy denies access unexpectedly?
Steps:

Check CloudTrail to see the denied operation and the exact error.

Use IAM Policy Simulator — simulate the user/role + action + resource to find which policy produced Deny.

Look for explicit Deny in any policy (identity policy, resource policy, SCP).

Check permission boundary — boundaries restrict maximum allowed actions.

Check session policies (if assumed role) or service control policies (Organizations).

Check resource-based policies (S3 bucket policy / KMS key policy / Lambda policy).

Check conditions like aws:SourceIp, aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent, kms:ViaService, etc.

Use AWS Access Analyzer / Trusted Advisor to find misconfigurations.

Tip: Explicit Deny always wins. Start by searching for Effect: "Deny" across policies.

9) What’s the difference between inline and managed IAM policies, and when would you use each?
Managed policies (AWS or Customer managed):

Re-usable. Create once, attach to many users/roles/groups.

Easier to update and version. AWS provides AWS-managed policies.

Recommended for most cases.

Inline policies:

Embedded directly in a single user, role, or group.

Tightly coupled and suited for one-off, highly specific permissions that should be deleted if the identity is deleted.

Harder to reuse and manage at scale.

When to use:

Managed: standard permissions, shareable policies, updates required across many identities.

Inline: rare cases where permissions must be uniquely tied to a single principal and should be removed with it (e.g., emergency/ephemeral access).

10) How do you ensure that developers can only launch specific EC2 instance types?
Answer: Use an IAM policy that denies ec2:RunInstances unless ec2:InstanceType matches allowed types.

Policy example (allow only t3.micro & t3.small):

{
  "Version":"2012-10-17",
  "Statement":[
    {
      "Effect":"Deny",
      "Action":"ec2:RunInstances",
      "Resource":"*",
      "Condition":{
        "StringNotEquals":{"ec2:InstanceType":["t3.micro","t3.small"]}
      }
    }
  ]
}
Notes: You must also ensure other related actions (CreateLaunchTemplate, etc.) are covered or restricted as needed. Test with policy simulator.

11) How do you restrict IAM users so they can log in only from your corporate IP range?
Answer: Use an IAM policy with an aws:SourceIp condition to Deny API/console access when the source IP is outside your allowed CIDR.

Example:

{
  "Version":"2012-10-17",
  "Statement":[{
    "Effect":"Deny",
    "Action":"*",
    "Resource":"*",
    "Condition":{
      "NotIpAddress":{"aws:SourceIp":"203.0.113.0/24"}
    }
  }]
}
Important: Browser-based Console sessions go through the public internet; aws:SourceIp works for console sign-in and API calls. For more robust access control, consider forcing access via a VPN or using AWS Client VPN / PrivateLink.

12) How do you grant cross-account access to Lambda functions?
Options:

Resource policy on the Lambda function — add aws lambda add-permission to allow an AWS principal from another account to InvokeFunction.

Cross-account role — have the other account assume a role that has lambda:InvokeFunction on the target function.

Example: Add permission to Lambda (allow account 111122223333 to invoke):

aws lambda add-permission \
  --function-name MyFunction \
  --statement-id AllowAccount1111 \
  --action lambda:InvokeFunction \
  --principal 111122223333
Best practice: Use resource-based policies for simple invoke permissions; use roles for more complex cross-account workflows.

13) What are IAM permission boundaries, and when do you use them?
Permission boundaries = a maximum permission limit applied to an IAM principal (user or role). Even if a user has an identity policy allowing an action, they cannot exceed the boundary.

Use cases:

Delegate permission management to teams but prevent privilege escalation (e.g., give devs ability to create roles but not admin).

Implement guardrails in multi-tenant environments.

How it works: Effective permissions = identity policy AND permission boundary (intersection).

Example: Set a boundary that only allows S3 and DynamoDB actions; even if the user later gets EC2 admin policy attached, EC2 actions are blocked.

14) How do you prevent users from deleting important S3 objects?
Options & examples:

Bucket policy deny deletes: add a bucket policy that denies s3:DeleteObject to everyone except a small admin role.

{
 "Version":"2012-10-17",
 "Statement":[{
   "Effect":"Deny",
   "Principal":"*",
   "Action":"s3:DeleteObject",
   "Resource":"arn:aws:s3:::important-bucket/*",
   "Condition":{"StringNotEquals":{"aws:PrincipalArn":"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/S3AdminRole"}}
 }]
}
Enable versioning + MFA Delete: Turn on versioning and enable MFA Delete — deletes must present MFA to permanently remove object versions. (Note: MFA Delete requires the root user and must be managed carefully.)

Use object lock (WORM) / retention policies: If you need immutable data for regulatory reasons, use S3 Object Lock (governance/compliance modes) to prevent deletion for a retention period.

Least privilege: Only give delete permissions to a tiny set of admins.

Best practice: Combine versioning + object lock or restricted bucket policy for critical data.

15) A new intern needs limited console access. How do you configure it?
Steps:

Create an IAM user (or better: use AWS SSO and a permission set).

Attach a restrictive policy that allows only the console actions needed — for example, view-only access for specific services, access to a training S3 bucket, or access to a sandbox account.

Use groups (e.g., Interns) and attach the policy to the group for easier management.

Set up MFA, short credential rotation, and an expiration process (disable after internship).

Use permission boundaries if interns might create resources — to cap resource creation.

Example policy giving read-only access to specific console resources:

{
 "Version":"2012-10-17",
 "Statement":[{
   "Effect":"Allow",
   "Action":[
     "s3:ListBucket","s3:GetObject",
     "ec2:DescribeInstances","logs:DescribeLogGroups"
   ],
   "Resource":"*"
 }]
}
Best practice: Put interns in isolated accounts (sandbox) using AWS Organizations and SSO to avoid accidental ops on production.

16) What’s the best way to assign permissions to hundreds of users at once?
Options (scale-friendly):

Use IAM groups and attach policies to groups (classic approach).

Use AWS Single Sign-On (IAM Identity Center) (recommended): manage central permission sets and map to identities (Okta/AD/IdP).

Automate with IaC: CloudFormation / Terraform to create and attach policies/roles consistently.

Use AWS Organizations + SCPs for account-level guardrails.

Best practice: Use groups or SSO permission sets OR automate with Terraform — don’t attach policies individually to users.

17) How do you monitor and detect unusual IAM activity?
Tools & signals:

AWS CloudTrail: log every API call — look for unusual CreateAccessKey, ConsoleLogin failures, AssumeRole from unusual regions.

CloudWatch Logs & Alarms: create metric filters for denied actions or suspicious patterns; alert on anomalies.

Amazon GuardDuty: identifies compromised credentials and unusual API calls.

AWS Config: rule drift, e.g., new policies attached or deletion.

IAM Access Analyzer: find publicly accessible resources or cross-account exposures.

AWS Security Hub: aggregate findings.

Automated detection: use ML or rules (failed logins, unusual source ip, excessive privilege escalations) and automated remediation (disable keys).

Example alerts to create: alert on creation of new IAM users, console login from new countries, creation of new access keys, or attachment of admin policies to non-admin users.

18) How do you allow federated users from an Active Directory/SSO provider to log in?
Answer: Use SAML or OIDC federation with AWS:

Options:

AWS IAM Identity Center (AWS SSO) — integrates with IdPs (Azure AD, Okta, ADFS) for central SSO management.

SAML federation (federate to IAM roles) — configure SAML provider in IAM and create roles with a trust policy that accepts SAML assertions from your IdP. Map AD groups to role ARNs.

OIDC — for providers supporting OIDC.

High-level flow: User logs into corporate IdP → IdP sends SAML assertion to AWS → AWS maps assertion to an IAM role → user receives temporary credentials and console/API access.

Best practice: Use AWS SSO/Identity Center for simplified management and provisioning.

19) How do you implement least privilege across multiple teams?
Approach (process + tech):

Role-based model: create narrow, job-role permission sets (role-per-application/team).

Permission boundaries: prevent escalation when teams manage their own roles.

Use AWS SSO permission sets and map to teams/groups.

Review & revoke cycle: regular access reviews, using Access Advisor data.

Use infrastructure-as-code to provision policies consistently and review PRs.

Use Guardrails (SCPs) to prevent dangerous cross-account actions.

Continuous monitoring with CloudTrail / IAM Access Analyzer.

Best practice: Start minimal and expand on request with justifications & approvals.

20) How do you revoke IAM access immediately if a user leaves the company?
Immediate steps (practical):

Disable user login: aws iam delete-login-profile --user-name alice (removes console login).

Delete or deactivate access keys: aws iam list-access-keys then aws iam update-access-key --access-key-id <id> --status Inactive or aws iam delete-access-key.

Remove from groups and detach policies: aws iam remove-user-from-group and aws iam detach-user-policy.

Delete or disable MFA device and delete-login-profile.

Revoke active sessions: For federated or SSO sessions, use AWS SSO console to revoke; for IAM user sessions, change password and/or delete session tokens. (Also rotate shared keys/roles if they were used.)

Audit: check CloudTrail for suspicious activity from that user.

If using IAM roles / SSO: disable corresponding SSO account / role assignment.

Example CLI to deactivate keys:

aws iam update-access-key --user-name alice --access-key-id AKIA... --status Inactive
Best practice: Have an offboarding playbook that automates these steps (runbook or automation) so human error doesn’t leave accounts active.

Short summary of the top recurring patterns / interview takeaways
Prefer temporary credentials (roles, STS) over long-lived access keys.

Use resource-based policies for cross-account and service-level permissions where applicable (Lambda, S3).

Use automation (AWS Config, Lambda, Terraform) to enforce rotation and large-scale changes.

Monitor aggressively (CloudTrail + GuardDuty + CloudWatch alerts).

Least privilege + permission boundaries + SCPs = defence-in-depth for multi-team orgs.

Practice offboarding automation to revoke access fast.
